# Assert.IQ pack — line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# The pack shipped without a .gitattributes. On Windows, Git's default
# core.autocrlf=true rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout, so a Windows
# clone produced shell scripts with CRLF line endings. Git Bash tolerates that,
# which is why it went unnoticed — but bash on Linux/WSL and macOS does not:
#
#   $ bash install.sh
#   install.sh: line 21: $'\r': command not found
#   /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# That breaks the documented "macOS / Linux / WSL" path for anyone whose
# checkout came from a Windows machine (a shared drive, a zip, a WSL session
# reading /mnt/c). Pinning the executable text formats here makes the checkout
# byte-correct regardless of the user's core.autocrlf setting.
#
# Existing clones do not re-normalize on their own. To fix one in place:
#   git add --renormalize . && git checkout -- .

# Let git decide for anything not named below.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts MUST be LF: the shebang and every line are parsed by bash.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# PowerShell is native to Windows; CRLF is its idiomatic form and both
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7+ accept either.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf
*.psd1          text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Python is read by the interpreter, which accepts either, but the pack's
# helpers are also sourced by shell one-liners. Keep them LF.
*.py            text eol=lf

# Data and docs: normalize in the repo, let the platform decide on checkout.
*.json          text
*.md            text
*.yaml          text
*.yml           text
*.jsonl         text eol=lf

# Never touch binaries.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.tar.gz        binary
*.zip           binary
